1. POST

POST is mainly used to create new data.

When you use POST:

The server usually generates the ID
A new resource is created
Multiple identical POST requests may create multiple records
Example

Request:

POST /users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Rahul"
}

Server response:

{
  "id": 101,
  "name": "Rahul"
}

The server created a new user.

2. PUT

PUT is mainly used to replace or update an existing resource.

When you use PUT:

The client usually knows the resource ID
Existing data gets replaced
Sending the same PUT request repeatedly gives the same result
Example

Request:

PUT /users/101
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Rahul Sharma"
}

This updates/replaces user 101.

Important Concept: Idempotent
PUT is idempotent

Meaning:

Sending the same request 1 time or 100 times → same final result

Example:

PUT /users/101
{
  "name": "Rahul"
}

No matter how many times you send it, user 101 still becomes "Rahul".

POST is NOT idempotent

Each request may create a new resource.

Example:

POST /orders

Sending it 3 times may create:

Order #1
Order #2
Order #3



